442-[Term]\n21443:id: PATO:0045089\n21444-name: normal sensitivity of a process to oxygen\n21445-def: \"A sensitivity of occurrent to oxygen which is relatively normal.\" []\n21446-subset: disposition_slim\n--\n21450-\n21451-[Term]\n21452:id: PATO:0045090\n21453-name: cardinal direction\n21454-def: \"The four cardinal directions or cardinal points are the directions north, east, south, and west, commonly denoted by their initials, N, E, S, W. East and west are at right angles to north and south, with east being in the clockwise direction of rotation from north and west being directly opposite east.\" [Wikipedia:http\\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cardinal_direction]\n21455-subset: value_slim\n--\n21459-\n21460-[Term]\n21461:id: PATO:0045091\n21462-name: north\n21463-def: \"North is one of the four compass points or cardinal directions. It is the opposite of south and is perpendicular to east and west.\" [Wikipedia:http\\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North]\n21464-subset: value_slim\n--\n21469-\n21470-[Term]\n21471:id: PATO:0045092\n21472-name: east\n21473-def: \"East is one of the four cardinal directions or points of the compass. It is the opposite direction from west.\" [Wikipedia:http\\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East]\n21474-subset: value_slim\n--\n21479-\n21480-[Term]\n21481:id: PATO:0045093\n21482-name: south\n21483-def: \"South is one of the four cardinal directions or compass points. South is the polar opposite of north and is perpendicular to east and west.\" [Wikipedia:http\\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South]\n21484-subset: value_slim\n--\n21489-\n21490-[Term]\n21491:id: PATO:0045094\n21492-name: west\n21493-def: \"West is one of the four cardinal directions or points of the compass. It is the opposite direction from east.\" [Wikipedia:http\\://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West]\n21494-subset: value_slim\n--\n21499-\n21500-[Term]\n21501:id: PATO:0050000\n21502-name: rate of occurence\n21503-def: \"The number of repeated events per unit time, occurring in a repeating series. (e.g. the number of heart beats occurring over 1 minute)\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9943-9018]\n21504-is_a: PATO:0000161 ! rate\n21505-\n21506-[Term]\n21507:id: PATO:0050001\n21508-name: rate of continuous process\n21509-def: \"The rate of output of a continuous process. Example include: growth rate, cell migration rate, enzyme reaction rate.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9943-9018]\n21510-is_a: PATO:0000161 ! rate\n--\n21512-\n21513-[Term]\n21514:id: PATO:0055001\n21515-name: decreased spatial extent of a process\n21516-def: \"A spatial distribution of a process in which the process occupies a smaller length, area, or volume than the reference process. For example, heterochromatin may form over a shorter length of a chromosome in a mutant than in wild type.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4148-4606]\n21517-is_a: PATO:0002302 ! decreased process quality\n--\n21521-\n21522-[Term]\n21523:id: PATO:0055002\n21524-name: increased spatial extent of a process\n21525-def: \"A spatial distribution of a process in which the process occupies a greater length, area, or volume than the reference process. For example, heterochromatin may form over a shorter length of a chromosome in a mutant than in wild type.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4148-4606]\n21526-is_a: PATO:0002304 ! increased process quality\n--\n21530-\n21531-[Term]\n21532:id: PATO:0055003\n21533-name: increased rate of occurrence\n21534-def: \"A rate of occurrence that is relatively high.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4148-4606]\n21535-is_a: PATO:0000912 ! increased rate\n--\n21539-\n21540-[Term]\n21541:id: PATO:0055004\n21542-name: decreased rate of occurrence\n21543-def: \"A rate of occurrence that is relatively low.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4148-4606]\n21544-is_a: PATO:0000911 ! decreased rate\n--\n21548-\n21549-[Term]\n21550:id: PATO:0055005\n21551-name: increased rate of continuous process\n21552-def: \"A rate of output of a continuous process that is relatively high.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4148-4606]\n21553-is_a: PATO:0000912 ! increased rate\n--\n21558-\n21559-[Term]\n21560:id: PATO:0055006\n21561-name: decreased rate of continuous process\n21562-def: \"A rate of output of a continuous process that is relatively low.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4148-4606]\n21563-is_a: PATO:0000911 ! decreased rate\n--\n21568-\n21569-[Term]\n21570:id: PATO:0060001\n21571-name: sex-specific\n21572-def: \"A quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's being present in one sex and absent from the other in members of the same species.\" [http://orcid.org/0000-0002-1373-1705]\n21573-is_a: PATO:0002451 ! sexually dimorphic\n--\n21576-\n21577-[Term]\n21578:id: PATO:0060002\n21579-name: male-specific\n21580-def: \"A quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's being present in males and absent from females in members of the same species.\" [http://orcid.org/0000-0002-1373-1705]\n21581-is_a: PATO:0060001 ! sex-specific\n--\n21584-\n21585-[Term]\n21586:id: PATO:0060003\n21587-name: female-specific\n21588-def: \"A quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's being present in females and absent from males in members of the same species.\" [http://orcid.org/0000-0002-1373-1705]\n21589-is_a: PATO:0060001 ! sex-specific\n--\n21592-\n21593-[Term]\n21594:id: PATO:0065001\n21595-name: lysed\n21596-def: \"The breakdown, disintegration, destruction of an entity due to the breaking of cell membranes by any means.\" [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lysis]\n21597-is_a: PATO:0000141 ! structure\n--\n21599-\n21600-[Term]\n21601:id: PATO:0065002\n21602-name: patchy pigmentation\n21603-def: \"A degree of pigmentation quality that is diversified with patches, or irregularity.\" [https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003224.htm]\n21604-is_a: PATO:0002248 ! pigmented\n--\n21606-\n21607-[Term]\n21608:id: PATO:0070000\n21609-name: sequential hermaphrodite\n21610-def: \"A hermaphroditic quality that inheres in a population or organism where an organism changes is a different sexes during different life stages.\" [http://orcid.org/0000-0002-2908-3327, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sequential_hermaphroditism, https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8815-0078]\n21611-comment: Sequential hermaphrodity is generally characterized by an organism producing female gametes and male gametes during different life stages.\n--\n21615-\n21616-[Term]\n21617:id: PATO:0070001\n21618-name: neurogliaform morphology\n21619-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have a small round soma, a large number of short, smooth, or slightly beaded primary dendrites that give rise to only a few secondary branches, and a branched axon that establishes a dense axonal mesh with thin shafts.\" [PMID:17122314]\n21620-xref: ilxtr:NeurogliaformPhenotype\n--\n21623-\n21624-[Term]\n21625:id: PATO:0070002\n21626-name: basket cell morphology\n21627-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in multipolar neurons with densely branched terminal axonal arborizations that form basket-like structures surrounding and synapsing to the somas of target cells.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4, WikipediaVersioned:Basket_cell&oldid=951703880]\n21628-xref: ILX:0101123\n--\n21633-\n21634-[Term]\n21635:id: PATO:0070003\n21636-name: small basket cell morphology\n21637-def: \"A basket cell morphology that inheres in neurons with axonal arbors composed of frequent, short, curvy axonal branches that tend to be near their somata and within the same layer.\" [PMID:11884355]\n21638-xref: ilxtr:SmallBasketPhenotype\n--\n21641-\n21642-[Term]\n21643:id: PATO:0070004\n21644-name: large basket cell morphology\n21645-def: \"A basket cell morphology that inheres in neurons that are large, and typically ascend to give rise to many long horizontally and vertically projecting axon collaterals that traverse neighboring columns and can extend through all cortical layers.\" [PMID:11884355]\n21646-xref: ilxtr:LargeBasketPhenotype\n--\n21649-\n21650-[Term]\n21651:id: PATO:0070005\n21652-name: nest basket cell morphology\n21653-def: \"A basket cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have simpler dendritic arbors (compared to small or large basket cell morphology), and an axonal plexus of intermediate density, composed of a few long, smooth axonal branches.\" [PMID:11884355]\n21654-xref: ilxtr:NestBasketPhenotype\n--\n21657-\n21658-[Term]\n21659:id: PATO:0070006\n21660-name: cortical bipolar morphology\n21661-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have two principal dendrites that emerge from the soma and begin to branch some distance from it, have few spines, and branch in narrow fields. This is in contrast to bitufted morphology where branching occurs close to the soma.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4, PMID:18568015]\n21662-comment: This is specific to cortical bipolar cells and are distinguished from retinal bipolar cell morphology which has an axon and dendrite emerging from opposite ends of the soma.\n--\n21668-\n21669-[Term]\n21670:id: PATO:0070007\n21671-name: Martinotti morphology\n21672-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which are large and non-spiny, with dendrites radiating in a multipolar or bitufted pattern, often favouring the descending side, and axons that ascend directly from the superficial side of the soma, and travel upward without branching.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21673-xref: ilxtr:MartinottiPhenotype\n--\n21676-\n21677-[Term]\n21678:id: PATO:0070008\n21679-name: T Martinotti morphology\n21680-def: \"A Martinotti cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have axons that form a horizontal ramification, making it T-shaped.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21681-synonym: \"'T-shaped' Martinotti morphology\" EXACT []\n--\n21684-\n21685-[Term]\n21686:id: PATO:0070009\n21687-name: fan Martinotti morphology\n21688-def: \"A Martinotti cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have axons that form a fan-like plexus.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21689-synonym: \"'fanning-out' Martinotti morphology\" EXACT []\n--\n21692-\n21693-[Term]\n21694:id: PATO:0070010\n21695-name: stellate morphology\n21696-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons that have dendritic processes radiating from the cell body forming a star-like shape.\" [ISBN:9780123973481]\n21697-xref: ILX:0111036\n--\n21701-\n21702-[Term]\n21703:id: PATO:0070011\n21704-name: chandelier cell morphology\n21705-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which are small and round, and make highly specialized synapses with the axon initial segments of pyramidal neurons, wrapping them with candle-shaped synaptic 'cartridges'.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21706-comment: Chandelier cells can be bitufted or multipolar. {xref=\"DOI:10.1016/B978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4\"}\n--\n21712-\n21713-[Term]\n21714:id: PATO:0070012\n21715-name: bitufted cell morphology\n21716-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have two clusters of primary dendrites that originate directly and extend from opposite ends of the soma, and axons that form a plexus which spreads widely. Compared to the narrow vertical axonal projection of bipolar morphology, and the 'horse-tail' axonal cluster of double boquet cells, bitufted morphology has wide horizontal axonal spans, and branching that occur close to the soma. Their vertical projection is also less extensive and crosses mostly to neighboring layers.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4, PMID:15378039, PMID:18568015]\n21717-xref: ilxtr:BituftedPhenotype\n--\n21720-\n21721-[Term]\n21722:id: PATO:0070013\n21723-name: spiny stellate cell morphology\n21724-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have spiny basal dendrites and resemble pyramidal cells but lose their apical dendrites as they mature. Their axons nearly always emerge from the descending side of the soma, but often recurve and ascend with arc-like collaterals.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21725-xref: ilxtr:SpinyStellatePhenotype\n--\n21728-\n21729-[Term]\n21730:id: PATO:0070015\n21731-name: pyramidal family morphology\n21732-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have a pyramidal shaped soma, a single axon, a large apical dendrite and multiple basal dendrites.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21733-xref: ILX:0109553\n--\n21737-\n21738-[Term]\n21739:id: PATO:0070016\n21740-name: horizontal pyramidal morphology\n21741-def: \"A pyramidal family morphology that inheres in neurons which have an apical tree which is oriented parallel to the pia.\" [PMID:30715238]\n21742-xref: ilxtr:HorizontalPyramidalPhenotype\n--\n21746-\n21747-[Term]\n21748:id: PATO:0070017\n21749-name: standard pyramidal morphology\n21750-def: \"A pyramidal family morphology that inheres in neurons which have a standard pyramidal shape with its apical dendrite aligned vertically.\" [doi:10.1016/b978-0-12-369497-3.10004-4]\n21751-synonym: \"pyramidal cell morphology\" EXACT []\n--\n21756-\n21757-[Term]\n21758:id: PATO:0070018\n21759-name: tufted pyramidal morphology\n21760-def: \"A pyramidal cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have a distinct tuft formation, distal from the soma.\" [PMID:30715238]\n21761-xref: ilxtr:TuftedPyramidalPhenotype\n--\n21764-\n21765-[Term]\n21766:id: PATO:0070019\n21767-name: untufted pyramidal morphology\n21768-def: \"A pyramidal cell morphology that inheres in neurons which lack a clear tuft formation but extend to large radial distances.\" [PMID:30715238]\n21769-xref: ilxtr:UntuftedPyramidalPhenotype\n--\n21772-\n21773-[Term]\n21774:id: PATO:0070020\n21775-name: stellate pyramidal morphology\n21776-def: \"A pyramidal cell morphology that inheres in neurons which lack a tuft formation but extend to small radial distances forming a star-like shape.\" [PMID:30715238]\n21777-xref: ilxtr:StarPyramidalPhenotype\n--\n21780-\n21781-[Term]\n21782:id: PATO:0070021\n21783-name: inverted pyramidal morphology\n21784-def: \"A pyramidal cell morphology that inheres in neurons which have an apical tree which is oriented towards the white matter.\" [PMID:30715238]\n21785-xref: ilxtr:InvertedPyramidalPhenotype\n--\n21788-\n21789-[Term]\n21790:id: PATO:0070025\n21791-name: unipolar neuron morphology\n21792-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons with only one process, a neurite, that extends from the cell body. The neurite then branches to form dendritic and axonal processes.\" [WikipediaVersioned:Unipolar_neuron&oldid=994458581]\n21793-is_a: PATO:0010006 ! cell morphology\n--\n21795-\n21796-[Term]\n21797:id: PATO:0070026\n21798-name: multipolar neuron morphology\n21799-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons which possess three or more neurites, usually a single axon and many dendrites and dendritic branches.\" [WikipediaVersioned:Multipolar_neuron&oldid=1022942606]\n21800-xref: ILX:0107196\n--\n21804-\n21805-[Term]\n21806:id: PATO:0070027\n21807-name: bitufted dendrite cell morphology\n21808-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons with dendrites that are bidirectional, emerging from opposite ends of the soma, with the two arbors extending in radial or tangential directions.\" [PMID:18568015]\n21809-comment: This morphology type is distinct from bitufted cell morphology in that it refers to bitufted dendrite morphology rather than bitufted cell morphology.\n--\n21812-\n21813-[Term]\n21814:id: PATO:0070028\n21815-name: extratelencephalic projecting\n21816-def: \"A neuron projection quality that is a characteristic of neurons whose soma is located in the telencephalon and that project to structures outside the telencephalon\" [DOI:10.1101/2020.10.19.343129]\n21817-synonym: \"ET projecting\" EXACT []\n--\n21821-\n21822-[Term]\n21823:id: PATO:0070029\n21824-name: corticothalamic projecting\n21825-def: \"A neuron projection quality that is a characteristic of neurons with soma in the cerebral cortex and projections to the thalamus.\" [DOI:10.1101/2020.10.19.343129]\n21826-synonym: \"CT projecting\" EXACT []\n--\n21830-\n21831-[Term]\n21832:id: PATO:0070030\n21833-name: near projecting\n21834-def: \"A neuron projection quality that is a characteristic of neurons with projections that terminate locally rather than distantly.\" [DOI:10.1101/2020.10.19.343129]\n21835-synonym: \"NP\" EXACT []\n--\n21839-\n21840-[Term]\n21841:id: PATO:0070031\n21842-name: corticomedulla projecting\n21843-def: \"A neuron projection quality that is a characteristic of neurons that project to the medulla oblongata from other brain regions.\" [DOI:10.1101/2020.10.19.343129]\n21844-synonym: \"MY projecting\" EXACT []\n--\n21847-\n21848-[Term]\n21849:id: PATO:0070033\n21850-name: neuron projection quality\n21851-def: \"A physical quality inhering in a neuron by virtue of the relative location of its soma and the regions to which it projects and makes synaptic contacts in.\" [DOI:10.1101/2020.10.19.343129]\n21852-is_a: PATO:0001018 ! physical quality\n--\n21854-\n21855-[Term]\n21856:id: PATO:0070034\n21857-name: intratelencephalic projecting\n21858-def: \"A neuron projection quality that is a characteristic of neurons with both soma and projections entirely within the telencephalon.\" [DOI:10.1101/2020.10.19.343129]\n21859-synonym: \"IT projecting\" EXACT []\n--\n21863-\n21864-[Term]\n21865:id: PATO:0070036\n21866-name: dendrite quality\n21867-def: \"A quality inhering in a neuron's dendrites by virtue of the bearer's physical composition.\" []\n21868-xref: ILX:0103022\n--\n21871-\n21872-[Term]\n21873:id: PATO:0070038\n21874-name: spiny dendrite quality\n21875-def: \"A dendrite quality inhering in a neuron by virtue of the bearer's dendrite having spines.\" []\n21876-xref: ILX:0110980\n--\n21879-\n21880-[Term]\n21881:id: PATO:0070039\n21882-name: tufted dendrite quality\n21883-def: \"A dendrite quality inhering in a neuron by virtue of the bearer's dendrite having a terminal tuft.\" [ILX:0112065]\n21884-xref: ILX:0112065\n--\n21888-\n21889-[Term]\n21890:id: PATO:0070040\n21891-name: aspiny dendrite quality\n21892-def: \"A dendrite quality inhering in a neuron by virtue of the bearer's dendrite having few or no recognizable dendritic spines.\" [ILX:0100932]\n21893-xref: ILX:0100932\n--\n21897-\n21898-[Term]\n21899:id: PATO:0070041\n21900-name: varicose dendrite quality\n21901-def: \"A dendrite quality inhering in a neuron by virtue of the bearer's dendrite having numerous swellings or varicosities along their length.\" [ILX:0112263]\n21902-xref: ILX:0112263\n--\n21906-\n21907-[Term]\n21908:id: PATO:0070042\n21909-name: retinal bipolar morphology\n21910-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in retinal neurons which have two principal fibres, an axon and a dendrite, that emerge from opposite end the soma.\" [WikipediaVersioned:Retina_bipolar_cell&oldid=1033254616]\n21911-comment: This is distinct from cortical bipolar morphology which has two dendrites emerging from opposite ends of the soma, rather than one axon and one dendrite.\n--\n21914-\n21915-[Term]\n21916:id: PATO:0070044\n21917-name: anatomical structure quality\n21918-def: \"A quality of continuant that exist at the anatomical level of organisation and anything under it. This includes, but is not limited to, cells , tissues, and components.\" []\n21919-is_a: PATO:0001241 ! physical object quality\n--\n21921-\n21922-[Term]\n21923:id: PATO:0070045\n21924-name: anatomical histological quality\n21925-def: \"A quality inhering in an anotomical structure by virtue of its capacity to be stained by specific histological dyes.\" []\n21926-is_a: PATO:0070044 ! anatomical structure quality\n--\n21928-\n21929-[Term]\n21930:id: PATO:0070046\n21931-name: neutrophillic\n21932-def: \"An affinity inhering in an anatomical structure by virtue of the bearer exhibiting a molecular interaction that stains and colors, pale-pink, with Wright-Giemsa stain.\" [GOC:add]\n21933-is_a: PATO:0070045 ! anatomical histological quality\n--\n21935-\n21936-[Term]\n21937:id: PATO:0070047\n21938-name: polychromatophilic\n21939-def: \"An affinity inhering in an anatomical structure by virtue of the bearer exhibiting a molecular interaction for both basic and acid stains under specific pH conditions.\" [GOC:add]\n21940-is_a: PATO:0070045 ! anatomical histological quality\n--\n21942-\n21943-[Term]\n21944:id: PATO:0070059\n21945-name: rusty\n21946-def: \"A texture quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's being covered by iron oxide as a result of oxidation.\" [WikipediaVersioned:Rust&oldid=1072363937]\n21947-is_a: PATO:0000150 ! texture\n--\n21949-\n21950-[Term]\n21951:id: PATO:0070060\n21952-name: quality of interaction of a substance with electromagnetic radiation\n21953-def: \"A quality of a substance on which or through which electromagnetic radiation impinges or traverses with respect to radiation. This substance quality changes the quality of the incident radiation with respect to any of: intensity, direction, scatter and wavelength.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5111-7263]\n21954-subset: attribute_slim\n--\n21956-\n21957-[Term]\n21958:id: PATO:0070061\n21959-name: double bouquet morphology\n21960-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons with bitufted dendritic morphology but with the special feature of a tight fascicular axonal cylinder that resembles a 'horse tail'.\" [PMID:15378039]\n21961-is_a: PATO:0070027 ! bitufted dendrite cell morphology\n21962-\n21963-[Term]\n21964:id: PATO:0070062\n21965-name: bistratified dendrite cell morphology\n21966-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons with dendrites that stratify at two and only two locations.\" [PMID:34093409]\n21967-is_a: PATO:0010006 ! cell morphology\n--\n21969-\n21970-[Term]\n21971:id: PATO:0070063\n21972-name: monostratified dendrite cell morphology\n21973-def: \"A cell morphology that inheres in neurons with dendrites that stratify at one and only one location.\" [PMID:34093409]\n21974-is_a: PATO:0010006 ! cell morphology\n--\n21976-\n21977-[Term]\n21978:id: PATO:0070064\n21979-name: dendritic field size\n21980-def: \"A 3-D extent quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the spatial region that is defined by the maximal span of the bearer's dendritic arbor.\" [https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7258-9596, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1373-1705, https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5260-9315]\n21981-is_a: PATO:0001710 ! 3-D extent\n--\n21985-\n21986-[Term]\n21987:id: PATO:0080001\n21988-name: quality of a fluid\n21989-def: \"A physical quality inhering in an entity exhibiting the physical charachteristics of a material that flows under applied force.\" [WikipediaVersioned:Fluid&oldid=1049126570]\n21990-subset: attribute_slim\n--\n21993-\n21994-[Term]\n21995:id: PATO:0085001\n21996-name: salinity\n21997-def: \"An osmolarity quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the total amount of ionic salts dissolved in the bearer.\" [http://db0nus869y26v.cloudfront.net/en/Salinity, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salinity, https://rwu.pressbooks.pub/webboceanography/chapter/5-3-salinity-patterns/, https://www.sciencedirect.com/referencework/9780080983004/treatise-on-geochemistry, https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780857096807500012]\n21998-is_a: PATO:0001655 ! osmolarity\n--\n22001-\n22002-[Term]\n22003:id: PATO:0095001\n22004-name: organismal area to mass ratio\n22005-def: \"Quotient (or ratio) of surface area of an organism divided by the mass of the same organism\" []\n22006-is_a: PATO:0001470 ! ratio\n--\n22010-\n22011-[Term]\n22012:id: PATO:0095002\n22013-name: cursorial\n22014-def: \"An organismal quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's morphological and physiological adaptation for running.\" []\n22015-is_a: PATO:0001995 ! organismal quality\n--\n22018-\n22019-[Term]\n22020:id: PATO:0095003\n22021-name: fossorial\n22022-def: \"An organismal quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's morphological and physiological adaptation for digging and living underground.\" []\n22023-is_a: PATO:0001995 ! organismal quality\n--\n22026-\n22027-[Term]\n22028:id: PATO:0095004\n22029-name: arboreal\n22030-def: \"An organismal quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's morphological and physiological adaptation for living in trees.\" []\n22031-is_a: PATO:0001995 ! organismal quality\n--\n22034-\n22035-[Term]\n22036:id: PATO:0095005\n22037-name: crepuscular\n22038-def: \"A quality of a process that happens during dawn and dusk.\" []\n22039-is_a: PATO:0001236 ! process quality\n--\n22042-\n22043-[Term]\n22044:id: PATO:0095006\n22045-name: nocturnal\n22046-def: \"A quality of a process that happens during the hours of darkness.\" []\n22047-is_a: PATO:0001236 ! process quality\n--\n22050-\n22051-[Term]\n22052:id: PATO:0095007\n22053-name: diurnal\n22054-def: \"A quality of a process that happens during the hours of daylight.\" []\n22055-is_a: PATO:0001236 ! process quality\n--\n22058-\n22059-[Term]\n22060:id: PATO:0095008\n22061-name: migratory\n22062-def: \"An organismal quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's morphological and physiological adaptation for participating in long-distance movement of conspecifics, usually on a seasonal basis.\" []\n22063-is_a: PATO:0001995 ! organismal quality\n--\n22066-\n22067-[Term]\n22068:id: PATO:0095009\n22069-name: anecic\n22070-def: \"An organismal quality inhering in a bearer by virtue of the bearer's ability to burrow more than 20 cm deep into the soil and make permanent, vertical burrows.\" []\n22071-is_a: PATO:0001995 ! organismal quality\n--\n22074-\n22075-[Term]\n22076:id: PATO:0103000\n22077-name: quantitative\n22078-def: \"A quality of an entity that can be represented numerically, including anything that can be counted, measured, or given a numerical value.\" [https://libguides.macalester.edu/c.php?g=527786&p=3608639, https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/data-glossary/quantitative-data]\n22079-is_a: PATO:0000001 ! quality\n--\n22081-\n22082-[Term]\n22083:id: PATO:0103001\n22084-name: total mass\n22085-def: \"A quantitative quality that represents the sum of all 'mass' qualities of the component parts that constitute a whole entity. The total mass quality inheres in the whole entity and reflects the aggregate measure of mass across all its components, such that changes in the mass of individual components may not necessarily correspond to equivalent changes in the total mass of the whole.\\n\\nExample of usage: \\\"total body mass\\\" represents the sum of the masses of all tissues and organs in the body, while \\\"liver mass\\\" represents only the mass of the liver. An increase in liver mass does not necessarily mean an increase in total body mass, as the mass of other organs or tissues may decrease simultaneously.\" []\n22086-is_a: PATO:0000125 ! mass\n--\n22089-\n22090-[Term]\n22091:id: PATO:0103002\n22092-name: increased total mass\n22093-def: \"A total mass which is higher than normal or average.\" []\n22094-is_a: PATO:0001563 ! increased mass\n--\n22098-\n22099-[Term]\n22100:id: PATO:0103003\n22101-name: decreased total mass\n22102-def: \"A total mass which is lower than normal or average.\" []\n22103-is_a: PATO:0001562 ! decreased mass\n--\n22107-\n22108-[Term]\n22109:id: PATO:0103004\n22110-name: total amount\n22111-def: \"A quantitative quality that represents the sum of all 'amount' qualities of the component parts that constitute a whole entity. The total amount quality inheres in the whole entity and reflects the aggregate measure across all its components, such that changes in the amount of individual components may not necessarily correspond to equivalent changes in the total amount of the whole.\\n\\nExample of usage: \\\"blood total protein amount\\\" represents the sum of the amounts of all protein types in blood, while \\\"blood leptin amount\\\" represents only the amount of leptin proteins. An increase in blood leptin amount does not necessarily mean an increase in blood total protein amount, as other protein levels may decrease simultaneously.\" []\n22112-is_a: PATO:0000070 ! amount\n--\n22115-\n22116-[Term]\n22117:id: PATO:0103005\n22118-name: decreased total amount\n22119-def: \"A total amount which is relatively low.\" []\n22120-is_a: PATO:0001997 ! decreased amount\n--\n22124-\n22125-[Term]\n22126:id: PATO:0103006\n22127-name: increased total amount\n22128-def: \"A total amount which is relatively high.\" []\n22129-is_a: PATO:0000470 ! increased amount\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
